    {
      "id": "LST-01",
      "chapter": "normalization",
      "title": "The coefficient denominator and the congruence of the two substituted arguments",
      "anchor": "normalization-log-substitution",
      "statement": "For a zero-constant logarithm of integral functional-equation type and n=q^k m>0 with q not dividing m, p^k f_n and p^k τ(f_n) are integral. For integral α, α(T)^q and (τα)(T^q) agree modulo p. Their nth powers agree modulo p^(1+hk) when q=p^h with h≥1.",
      "hypotheses": "A characteristic-zero ultrametric field with |p|<1, prime p, q=p^h for h≥1, and a coefficient endomorphism inducing the q-power operation modulo p on the valuation ring. HTC uses the complete unramified field, τ=σ² and h=2.",
      "definitions": [
        "the integral-error condition Δf=f(T)−(τf)(T^q)/p",
        "the recurrence at indices divisible by q",
        "coefficientwise divisibility of a power series",
        "the binomial gain when raising congruent integral arguments to the pth power"
      ],
